From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 05:38:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 032/185] segacd: the CDD handover, read out of the BIOS
 instead of guessed at

The user asked why I was guessing at what the BIOS does rather than
reading it. The answer is that I should have been reading it.

The sub-CPU CDBIOS is compressed inside the console's BIOS ROM, so
there is nothing to disassemble there -- but the boot process
decompresses it into PRG-RAM $0000-$5FFF, which is exactly the region
the E-A1 audit payload already snapshots into work RAM for the test
harness to dump. Sega's CDD routine falls straight out of it:

    btst   #1,$FF8037     wait for DRS: the packet is ready
    lea    $FF8038,a1     read ten status bytes
    lea    $FF8042,a1     write ten command bytes
    move.b #4,$FF8037     hand it over and re-arm

The last write is what was missing. There is no trigger among the
command registers -- four discs went looking for one -- because the
exchange is handed over by writing the control register back to
HOCK-only, clearing DTS and DRS. This driver never touched that
register after boot, so the gate array waited for an acknowledgement
that never came, no command was ever carried, and the drive reported
its own status while answering "invalid" to questions it had never
been asked.

The wait was also backwards: DRS set means the packet has arrived, not
that the bus is busy, so the previous code declined to send in exactly
the window the BIOS sends in.

The six-strategy search goes with it, since there is now one known
sequence, but its judge stays: a round trip counts only when the drive
names the sub-command it was asked for, twice, for two different
sub-commands.
